.TH CLADENC-RESUME 1 "" cladenc "cladenc"
.SH NAME
cladenc\-resume \- continue a directed evolution that stopped before its end
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBcladenc resume\fP
    [\fB\-\-help\fP]
    \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcladenc resume\fP continues a directed evolution that stopped before its
end, with the values that \fBcladenc start\fP was given, and prints the
directory of the best harness on standard output once the directed evolution
has ended, and nothing else there.
It says on standard error what it is doing.
It is stopped in the way \fBcladenc start\fP is, and then prints no report.
.PP
Before it does anything else, it removes the sandboxes that the directed
evolution left running, and then builds the environment of every task again.
It then starts again each diversification, that of an experiment among them,
and each trial that was under way when the directed evolution stopped, and
carries the directed evolution on to its end, as \fBcladenc start\fP does.
\fBcladenc\-start\fP(1) says how a directed evolution is carried out.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Print help.
.TP
\fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
The outputs directory of the directed evolution to continue.
.SH SANDBOXES LEFT RUNNING
A cladenc that is killed outright, as by \fBkill \-9\fP, ends none of its
sandboxes, and each goes on until its time limit has passed.
Before it does anything else, \fBcladenc resume\fP removes the sandboxes that
the directed evolution left running, so that it never works beside one of
them.
.PP
Every sandbox of a directed evolution is a Docker container, and carries a
Docker label, a name and a value that Docker keeps with a container:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cladenc.directed\-evolution=<DIR>
.EE
.RE
.PP
where <DIR> is the path of the outputs directory, with every symbolic link in
it resolved.
Once it has the outputs directory to itself, \fBcladenc resume\fP asks Docker
for the sandboxes that carry the label of its directed evolution, removes
them, and says how many it removed.
Where Docker cannot be asked, it exits with status 2 and continues nothing.
.PP
Where the directed evolution is not resumed, its sandboxes are removed by
hand.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
docker ps \-\-filter label=cladenc.directed\-evolution=<DIR>
.EE
.RE
.PP
lists them, their names begin cladenc\-, and \fBdocker kill\fP with a name
ends that sandbox.
Docker removes a sandbox of cladenc once it has ended.
.SH ENVIRONMENTS
Once the sandboxes are removed, \fBcladenc resume\fP builds the environment
of every task again, from the copy of the task in the outputs directory and
under the name it was given when the directed evolution was started, before it
brings up any sandbox.
The image that the Dockerfile of each task starts from has to be on the
machine: Docker fetches one that is not, from the registry of its name.
A task whose environment is not built stops \fBcladenc resume\fP with exit
status 2, and environments/<DATASET>/<TASK> in the outputs directory keeps
what Docker printed.
.PP
\fBcladenc resume\fP reads the task.toml of the copy of each task again, and
the variables it names as they are set where \fBcladenc resume\fP runs.
For a task whose task.toml names an image it builds nothing, and looks for the
image on the machine.
A task whose image is not there, or which takes a value from a variable that
is not set, stops \fBcladenc resume\fP with exit status 2.
.SH REPORT
When the directed evolution that it continued ends, \fBcladenc resume\fP
prints on standard error what \fBcladenc report\fP prints, after the line that
says that the directed evolution finished, and prints on standard output the
directory of the best harness and nothing else.
Of a directed evolution that had ended already, it prints the same after a
line that says that the directed evolution had finished.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcladenc\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-report\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-start\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-status\fP(1)
